Q: How do I make this function NOT return a flow&lt;string&gt;? A: <p>You shouldn't and you can't.</p>
<p>You <strong>shouldn't</strong> only return a <code>String</code> instead of a <code>Flow&lt;String&gt;</code> because the DataStore itself only exposes a Flow and this is what you would normally use and expect from it so you can be easily notified when something changes. If somewhere later in the call chain the current value is needed - <em>without</em> observing the Flow for changes - then just call <code>first()</code> on the Flow. That will return the current value.</p>
<p>You <strong>can't</strong> make the function non-suspending since the DataStore needs a coroutine to set everything up and read the data from disk by moving the heavy payload to another thread. Well, technically you can wrap everything in a <code>runBlocking</code> block, but that will defy the principles of structured concurrency and can lead to severe issues, making your app non-responsive or even crashing it. As a rule of thumb, only use it when you need interop with legacy Java code that isn't coroutine-aware or as an entry point for your code, like the <code>main</code> method or a unit test (although there are better alternatives available for both). In an Android app, for example, you will <em>never</em> have a good reason to use <code>runBlocking</code>.</p>
<p>Instead of fighting it, embrace Flows and coroutines. You will have much less friction. Let's call the function where the preference is needed <code>myFunction()</code>, then do either of these:</p>
<ol>
<li><p>Make <code>myFunction()</code> suspending.</p>
</li>
<li><p>Pass a CoroutineScope to <code>myFunction()</code> so you can launch a new coroutine there:</p>
<pre><code>fun CoroutineScope.myFunction() {
    launch {
        val preference = dataStore.getPrefIndependent(&quot;myKey&quot;).first()
        // do something with preference here
    }

    // whatever you do here runs asynchronously to the above, maybe even in parallel
}
</code></pre>
</li>
<li><p>Pass the preference as a <code>String</code> parameter to <code>myFunction()</code> and retrieve the preference beforehand, somewhere where you <em>do</em> have a coroutine or scope available. That allows <code>myFunction()</code> itself to stay non-suspending.</p>
</li>
</ol>

Q: What is the equivalent of ToString(&quot;00.00000&quot;) in C++? A: <p>The equivalent in C++ would be:</p>

<pre><code>#include &lt;iostream&gt;
#include &lt;iomanip&gt;

int main()
{
    std::cout &lt;&lt; std::setw(10) &lt;&lt; std::setfill('0') 
              &lt;&lt; std::fixed    &lt;&lt; std::setprecision(7) &lt;&lt; 7.0;
    return 0;
}
</code></pre>

<p>Output:</p>

<pre><code>07.0000000
</code></pre>

<p>If you need to actually store it into a <code>std::string</code>, then:</p>

<pre><code>#include &lt;sstream&gt;

std::ostringstream oss;
// ...
std::string s = oss.str();
</code></pre>

Q: Can&#39;t save data by index into mysql database in codeigniter A: <p><code>$data</code> should be a 2-dimensional array, so you need to push each array onto it in the loop. And the nested arrays need the index of the row to update.</p>
<p>When you call <code>update_batch()</code> you have to specify which column to use in the <code>WHERE</code> clause.</p>
<p>You don't need <code>[$key]</code>. <code>$value</code> is already the array element indexed by that key.</p>
<pre><code>foreach($query as $value){
    $data[] = array(
        'id' =&gt; $value['id'],
        'branch_price' =&gt; (float)$value['bprice'] * 1.30
    );
}

return $this-&gt;db-&gt;update_batch('items',$data, 'id');
</code></pre>
<p>Replace <code>id</code> with the actual name of the primary key of the table.</p>
<p>You can also have MySQL perform the calculation while updating instead of doing separate <code>SELECT</code> and <code>UPDATE</code> queries.</p>
<pre><code>$this-&gt;db-&gt;set('branch_price', 'branch_price * 1.30', false);
$this-&gt;db-&gt;update('items');
</code></pre>
<p>The <code>false</code> argument disables escaping, so the formula will be used directly.</p>

Q: How to add where condition to eloquent model on relation? A: <p>Relationships are implemented using additional queries. They are not part of the base query, and do not have access to modify the base query, so you cannot do this inside the relationship method.</p>

<p>The best way to do this is with a query scope:</p>

<p>Class:</p>

<pre><code>class Post extends Model
{
    public function scopeWithCategory($query)
    {
        return $query-&gt;with('category')-&gt;where('status', 'published');
    }
}
</code></pre>

<p>Query:</p>

<pre><code>$posts = Post::where('slug', $slug)-&gt;withCategory()-&gt;get();
</code></pre>

<h2>Edit</h2>

<p>Based on your comment, I think you've probably asked the wrong question. You may want to post another question explaining what you have setup, and what you need to do, and see if anyone has any suggestions from there.</p>

<p>However, to answer this specific question, I believe you should be able to do this using a <a href="https://laravel.com/docs/5.2/eloquent#global-scopes" rel="nofollow">global query scope</a>. This is different than a local scope described in my original answer above.</p>

<p>Global query scopes are applied when <code>get()</code> is called on the Eloquent query builder. They have access to the query builder object, and can see the items that have been requested to be eager loaded. Due to this, you should be able to create a global query scope that checks if <code>category</code> is to be eager loaded, and if so, add in the <code>status</code> constraint.</p>

<pre><code>class Post extends Model
{

    /**
     * The "booting" method of the model.
     *
     * @return void
     */
    protected static function boot()
    {
        // make sure to call the parent method
        parent::boot();

        static::addGlobalScope('checkCategory', function(\Illuminate\Database\Eloquent\Builder $builder) {
            // get the relationships that are to be eager loaded
            $eagers = $builder-&gt;getEagerLoads();

            // check if the "category" relationship is to be eager loaded.
            // if so, add in the "status" constraint.
            if (array_key_exists('category', $eagers)) {
                $builder-&gt;where('status', 'published');
            }
        });
    }
}
</code></pre>

<p>The code above shows adding in a global scope using an anonymous function. This was done for ease and clarity. I would suggest creating the actual scope class, as described in the documentation linked above.</p>

Q: Formatting a string with XML and XSLT like sprintf() in PHP A: <p>As example, this <code>index.xml</code>:</p>

<pre><code>&lt;index&gt; 
    &lt;locale&gt;en&lt;/locale&gt; 
    &lt;name&gt;ipalaus&lt;/name&gt; 
&lt;/index&gt; 
</code></pre>

<p>And this <code>index.en.xml</code>:</p>

<pre><code>&lt;language&gt;
    &lt;welcome&gt;Welcome &lt;name/&gt;&lt;/welcome&gt;
&lt;/language&gt;
</code></pre>

<p>Then, this stylesheet:</p>

<pre><code>&lt;xsl:stylesheet version="1.0" xmlns:xsl="http://www.w3.org/1999/XSL/Transform"&gt;
    &lt;xsl:param name="pLayoutURI" select="'index.en.xml'"/&gt;
    &lt;xsl:variable name="vData" select="/index"/&gt;
    &lt;xsl:variable name="vLayout" select="document($pLayoutURI,/)/language"/&gt;
    &lt;xsl:template match="/"&gt;
        &lt;html&gt;
            &lt;h1&gt;&lt;xsl:apply-templates select="$vLayout/welcome"/&gt;&lt;/h1&gt;
        &lt;/html&gt;
    &lt;/xsl:template&gt;
    &lt;xsl:template match="language/*/*[not(node())]"&gt;
        &lt;xsl:value-of select="$vData/*[name()=name(current())]"/&gt;
    &lt;/xsl:template&gt;
&lt;/xsl:stylesheet&gt;
</code></pre>

<p>Output:</p>

<pre><code>&lt;html&gt;
    &lt;h1&gt;Welcome ipalaus&lt;/h1&gt;
&lt;/html&gt;
</code></pre>

<p><strong>EDIT</strong>: This stylesheet</p>

<pre><code>&lt;xsl:stylesheet version="1.0" xmlns:xsl="http://www.w3.org/1999/XSL/Transform"&gt;
    &lt;xsl:param name="pLayoutURI" select="'index.en.xml'"/&gt;
    &lt;xsl:variable name="vLayout" select="document($pLayoutURI,/)/language"/&gt;
    &lt;xsl:template match="/index"&gt;
        &lt;ul&gt;
            &lt;xsl:apply-templates/&gt;
        &lt;/ul&gt;
    &lt;/xsl:template&gt;
    &lt;xsl:template match="video"&gt;
        &lt;li&gt;
            &lt;video src="{url}"&gt;
                &lt;xsl:attribute name="title"&gt;
                    &lt;xsl:apply-templates select="$vLayout/video/made/node()"
                                         mode="populate"&gt;
                        &lt;xsl:with-param name="pContext" select="."/&gt;
                    &lt;/xsl:apply-templates&gt;
                &lt;/xsl:attribute&gt;
            &lt;/video&gt;
        &lt;/li&gt;
    &lt;/xsl:template&gt;
    &lt;xsl:template match="language//*[not(node())]" mode="populate"&gt;
        &lt;xsl:param name="pContext" select="/.."/&gt;
        &lt;xsl:value-of select="$pContext/*[name()=name(current())]"/&gt;
    &lt;/xsl:template&gt;
&lt;/xsl:stylesheet&gt;
</code></pre>

<p>With this input:</p>

<pre><code>&lt;index&gt;
    &lt;video&gt;
        &lt;author&gt;ipalaus&lt;/author&gt;
        &lt;url&gt;ipalaus.mpg&lt;/url&gt;
    &lt;/video&gt;
    &lt;video&gt;
        &lt;author&gt;Alejandro&lt;/author&gt;
        &lt;url&gt;Alejandro.mpg&lt;/url&gt;
    &lt;/video&gt;
&lt;/index&gt;
</code></pre>

<p>And this external source <code>index.en.xml</code>:</p>

<pre><code>&lt;language&gt;
    &lt;video&gt;
        &lt;made&gt;This videos is made by &lt;author/&gt;&lt;/made&gt;
        &lt;random&gt;Another string&lt;/random&gt;
    &lt;/video&gt;
&lt;/language&gt;
</code></pre>

<p>Output:</p>

<pre><code>&lt;ul&gt;
   &lt;li&gt;
      &lt;video src="ipalaus.mpg" title="This videos is made by ipalaus"/&gt;
   &lt;/li&gt;
   &lt;li&gt;
      &lt;video src="Alejandro.mpg" title="This videos is made by Alejandro"/&gt;
   &lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
</code></pre>
